        'slug' => 'core-web-vitals-budget-ci',
        'title' => 'Enforcing a Core Web Vitals budget in CI, not in a retrospective',
        'type' => 'Playbook', 'topic' => 'Performance', 'accent' => 'blue',
        'author' => 'Daniel Osei', 'author_role' => 'Chief Technology Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 9, 'featured' => 1, 'published_at' => '2026-08-18',
        'excerpt' => 'A performance budget that lives in a slide deck is a wish. This is the exact pipeline configuration we use to make a regression fail the build instead of the quarter.',
        'body' => "<p>Almost every performance programme we inherit has the same shape: a budget was agreed, a dashboard was built, and nine months later the site is slower than when the programme started. The budget was never enforced anywhere that could stop a regression from shipping.</p><h2>Put the budget where commits die</h2><p>A budget only works if violating it is inconvenient at exactly the moment the violation is introduced. That means the pipeline, on the pull request, before review.</p><p>We run a synthetic Lighthouse pass against a preview deployment on every pull request, with a throttled 4G profile and a mid-range Android CPU multiplier. Three metrics are hard gates: Largest Contentful Paint, Interaction to Next Paint and Cumulative Layout Shift. A regression beyond the threshold fails the check, and the check is required.</p><h2>Set thresholds against the device you actually serve</h2><p>The most common mistake is measuring on the laptop the developer is using. Our default profile assumes a mid-range Android on a congested mobile network, because that is the median session for most of our clients. If the budget passes there, the desktop experience takes care of itself.</p><h2>Budget the bytes, not just the timings</h2><p>Timing metrics are noisy on shared CI runners. We pair them with deterministic byte budgets — total JavaScript, total CSS, largest single image — which never flake and catch most regressions before the timing gate would.</p><h2>Make the failure legible</h2><p>A failed check that says <code>LCP 2.9s &gt; 2.0s</code> teaches nobody anything. Ours posts the filmstrip, the largest contentful element, and a diff of the byte budgets against the base branch. The engineer who caused the regression can usually see the cause without opening a profiler.</p><h2>Allow deliberate exceptions, record them</h2><p>Occasionally a business reason justifies exceeding the budget. The override is a labelled exemption with an owner and an expiry date, reviewed monthly. Exemptions without an expiry are how budgets quietly die.</p>",
    ],
