        'slug' => 'strangler-migration-without-outage',
        'title' => 'Strangler migrations: how to replace a core system without an outage',
        'type' => 'Field notes', 'topic' => 'Architecture', 'accent' => 'violet',
        'author' => 'Daniel Osei', 'author_role' => 'Chief Technology Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 12, 'featured' => 1, 'published_at' => '2026-07-30',
        'excerpt' => 'Shadow running, domain-by-domain cutover and reconciliation windows — the operational detail that separates a strangler migration that works from one that gets abandoned.',
        'body' => "<p>The strangler pattern is well known and frequently attempted. Most attempts we are called in to rescue failed for operational reasons rather than architectural ones. The pattern is correct; the execution detail is where programmes die.</p><h2>Shadow before you switch</h2><p>Run the new system against real production traffic, computing the same results, writing nowhere. Reconcile every result automatically and treat any divergence as a defect. On our last settlement migration we shadowed for nine weeks and did not move a single transaction until divergence held at zero through a full month-end close.</p><h2>Cut over by domain, never by date</h2><p>A date-driven cutover forces every domain to be ready simultaneously, which means the least-ready domain sets the risk for all of them. Domain-driven cutover gives each one its own readiness bar, its own rollback and its own reconciliation window.</p><h2>Rehearse the rollback more than the rollout</h2><p>Teams rehearse going live. Almost nobody rehearses going back. We rehearse rollback in production-like conditions until it is boring, because that is the decision you will be making at three in the morning under pressure.</p><h2>Reconcile after cutover, not just before</h2><p>Keep the old system computing in shadow for at least one full business cycle after traffic moves. It costs a little infrastructure and it is the difference between finding a discrepancy in an automated report and finding it in a regulator's letter.</p><h2>Move the pager early</h2><p>The receiving team should take primary on-call while the delivery team is still present. A handover that happens after the consultants leave is not a handover, it is an abandonment.</p>",
    ],
