        'slug' => 'design-systems-engineers-adopt',
        'title' => 'Why engineers ignore your design system, and how to fix it',
        'type' => 'Essay', 'topic' => 'Design', 'accent' => 'violet',
        'author' => 'Mei Lin Chen', 'author_role' => 'Chief Design Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 8, 'featured' => 0, 'published_at' => '2026-06-22',
        'excerpt' => 'Adoption is not a communication problem. It is almost always a distribution problem, and it is usually solvable in a sprint.',
        'body' => "<p>When a design system goes unused, the diagnosis is usually that engineers need better onboarding. In our experience that is almost never the cause. Engineers ignore design systems that are more expensive to use than to bypass.</p><h2>Ship it as a dependency, not a document</h2><p>If adopting a component means reading a specification and reimplementing it, the specification is competing with a fifteen-minute local implementation and losing. Components must arrive as code that installs.</p><h2>Version it like software</h2><p>Semantic versioning, a changelog and a deprecation policy. A design system that changes without warning teaches teams to pin and forget.</p><h2>Make the accessible path the default path</h2><p>If the accessible variant requires extra configuration, the inaccessible one will ship. Roles, keyboard behaviour and focus management belong inside the component.</p><h2>Instrument adoption</h2><p>Measure which components are used, by which surfaces, at which versions. Low adoption of one component is a design problem worth investigating; low adoption everywhere is a distribution problem.</p><h2>Fund the maintenance</h2><p>A design system with no maintainer is a liability with a nice Figma file attached. Adoption tracks maintenance almost linearly.</p>",
    ],
    [
        'slug' => 'multi-tenant-before-first-enterprise-deal',
        'title' => 'Get tenancy right before your first enterprise deal, not after',
        'type' => 'Playbook', 'topic' => 'Architecture', 'accent' => 'blue',
        'author' => 'Elena Vasquez', 'author_role' => 'Chief Executive Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 7, 'featured' => 0, 'published_at' => '2026-05-14',
        'excerpt' => 'Retrofitting isolation, residency and entitlements costs three to five times what modelling them early does. Here is the minimum you need before the first contract.',
        'body' => "<p>Nearly every SaaS platform we are asked to fix has the same story: tenancy was added after the first enterprise contract. The retrofit typically costs three to five times what modelling it correctly at the start would have.</p><h2>The minimum viable tenancy model</h2><p>A tenant identifier on every row, enforced at the data layer rather than in application code. Row-level security or an equivalent guarantee means a missing <code>WHERE</code> clause cannot leak another customer's data.</p><h2>Residency is a data-model decision</h2><p>If a customer may one day require their data to stay in a region, that has to be expressible in the schema. Adding it later means a migration under contractual pressure.</p><h2>Entitlements are data, not conditionals</h2><p>Plan gating written as branching logic means every packaging change is a release. Entitlements stored as data mean a new plan is a configuration change and a sales conversation, not an engineering project.</p><h2>Audit from day one</h2><p>Append-only audit logging is cheap to add at the start and expensive to backfill. Enterprise buyers ask for it, and you cannot retroactively generate history you never wrote.</p>",
