    [
        'slug' => 'accessibility-as-engineering-constraint',
        'title' => 'Accessibility is an engineering constraint, not a compliance chore',
        'type' => 'Essay', 'topic' => 'Design', 'accent' => 'teal',
        'author' => 'Mei Lin Chen', 'author_role' => 'Chief Design Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 6, 'featured' => 0, 'published_at' => '2026-04-28',
        'excerpt' => 'Treating WCAG as a launch-week audit guarantees a bad result. Treating it as a build constraint costs almost nothing and improves the product for everyone.',
        'body' => "<p>The difference between teams that ship accessible products and teams that do not is almost never expertise. It is when in the process the constraint is applied.</p><h2>A late audit finds problems you cannot afford to fix</h2><p>An audit two weeks before launch surfaces structural issues — heading hierarchy, focus order, form semantics — that require rework nobody has time for. The findings become a backlog nobody clears.</p><h2>Gate the pipeline instead</h2><p>Automated checks catch perhaps forty percent of issues, but they catch them on the commit that introduced them, when the fix takes minutes. The remaining sixty percent needs manual testing, which is why we schedule it every sprint rather than once.</p><h2>Design with the constraint visible</h2><p>Contrast ratios, target sizes and focus states belong in the design file. A design that cannot pass is a design that will not pass, and finding out in code is the expensive way.</p><h2>The general benefit is real</h2><p>Larger targets help everyone on a phone. Clear focus states help keyboard power users. Plain error messages reduce support contacts. Accessibility work is usability work with a floor under it.</p>",
    ],
