        'slug' => 'measuring-what-transformation-delivers',
        'title' => 'Measuring what a transformation programme actually delivered',
        'type' => 'Field notes', 'topic' => 'Delivery', 'accent' => 'amber',
        'author' => 'Rashid Al-Amin', 'author_role' => 'Chief Delivery Officer',
        'read_minutes' => 9, 'featured' => 0, 'published_at' => '2026-03-19',
        'excerpt' => 'Most programmes report activity. Here is the small set of measures we publish to every client, including the ones that make us look bad.',
        'body' => "<p>Transformation programmes are unusually good at reporting activity and unusually bad at reporting outcomes. Story points delivered, sprints completed and milestones hit tell you a team was busy, not that a business changed.</p><h2>Publish four delivery measures</h2><p>Change failure rate, mean time to recovery, deployment frequency and lead time for change. They are well understood, hard to game and directly connected to the thing clients actually want, which is the ability to change their business quickly and safely.</p><h2>Publish the business measure you were hired for</h2><p>Every engagement has one number that justified it. Cost per transaction, revenue per session, task completion rate, time to first appointment. That number goes on the first page of every report, including the months it moves the wrong way.</p><h2>Publish the misses</h2><p>We report fleet SLOs to every client monthly, including the months we miss them. A partner who only reports good months is not giving you information, they are giving you marketing.</p><h2>Set the baseline before you start</h2><p>The most common reason a programme cannot prove value is that nobody measured the starting point. Baseline measurement is a discovery deliverable, not a nice-to-have.</p>",
