        'slug' => 'migrating-a-legacy-site-safely',
        'title' => 'Migrating a legacy site without losing your search rankings',
        'type' => 'Guide', 'topic' => 'Growth', 'accent' => 'amber',
        'author' => 'James Whitfield', 'author_role' => 'Managing Director, Americas',
        'read_minutes' => 8, 'featured' => 0, 'published_at' => '2026-02-11',
        'excerpt' => 'The URL mapping, redirect strategy and verification sequence that keeps organic traffic intact through a replatform — and what the Advanced Installer automates.',
        'body' => "<p>Replatforming is where organic traffic goes to die, almost always for the same three reasons: URLs changed without redirects, structured data was lost, and nobody verified the result until traffic had already fallen.</p><h2>Inventory before you design</h2><p>Export every indexed URL, every URL with an inbound link, and every URL with organic traffic in the last twelve months. That union is your migration scope. Anything not in it can change freely.</p><h2>Map one to one where you can</h2><p>Every old URL should resolve to the single closest new equivalent with a 301. Mapping many URLs to a homepage is the most common and most damaging shortcut.</p><h2>Rewrite content references, not just routes</h2><p>Internal links, canonical tags, sitemap entries and structured data all carry absolute URLs. The Advanced Installer's migration step rewrites these automatically when it imports content, which removes the error most manual migrations make.</p><h2>Verify in a fixed sequence</h2><ol><li>Crawl the new site and confirm no unexpected 404s.</li><li>Crawl the old URL list and confirm every one returns a single 301 to a 200.</li><li>Confirm canonical tags point at the new URLs, not the old ones.</li><li>Submit the new sitemap and monitor coverage daily for four weeks.</li></ol><h2>Do not change design and platform and information architecture at once</h2><p>If traffic falls you will not know which change caused it. Sequence them, even if it costs a few weeks.</p>",
